WEBVTT

STYLE
::cue(c.color9CD5FF) { color: #9CD5FF; }

1
00:00:01.000 --> 00:00:07.000 line:5% align:center
<b><c.color9CD5FF>One Pace Addon</c></b>

2
00:01:45.760 --> 00:01:47.340
"Steer for the Clouds!

3
00:01:47.380 --> 00:01:49.600
Capture the South Bird!"

4
00:01:49.760 --> 00:01:54.680
<b>Let's Eat</b>

5
00:01:54.600 --> 00:01:57.060
What did you
want to tell us, Old‐timer?

6
00:01:57.100 --> 00:01:58.730
Listen up and listen good.

7
00:01:58.770 --> 00:02:00.940
I've decided to tell you
guys everything I know

8
00:02:00.980 --> 00:02:03.270
about the sky island.

9
00:02:03.320 --> 00:02:05.070
Most of this is
rumor and hearsay,

10
00:02:05.130 --> 00:02:06.510
so you'll have to
make up your own minds

11
00:02:06.550 --> 00:02:08.130
whether you believe it or not.

12
00:02:08.170 --> 00:02:09.380
Mm. I believe it!

13
00:02:09.440 --> 00:02:10.400
Quiet!

14
00:02:10.440 --> 00:02:12.150
There's something
very strange that happens

15
00:02:12.190 --> 00:02:14.280
in this part of the ocean.

16
00:02:14.320 --> 00:02:16.460
Everything will suddenly
turn black as night,

17
00:02:16.460 --> 00:02:18.630
right in the middle
of the brightest day!

18
00:02:18.640 --> 00:02:19.340
Hey, yeah!

19
00:02:19.340 --> 00:02:21.170
The same thing happened to us!

20
00:02:21.220 --> 00:02:22.010
Right?

21
00:02:22.100 --> 00:02:23.770
Yeah! It was like
the sun vanished

22
00:02:23.810 --> 00:02:26.520
and then these enormous
monsters showed up!

23
00:02:28.440 --> 00:02:29.530
You saw the giants?

24
00:02:29.530 --> 00:02:31.370
There's a tale about
where they come from,

25
00:02:31.370 --> 00:02:34.200
but forget about 'em for now,
we can go into it later.

26
00:02:34.240 --> 00:02:37.160
Now, about this
strange nightfall.

27
00:02:39.270 --> 00:02:42.890
It's caused by the shadow
of an extremely dense cloud.

28
00:02:43.100 --> 00:02:44.670
Cumulonimbus clouds?

29
00:02:44.670 --> 00:02:47.380
But it was too dark for clouds,
it was like the sun went out!

30
00:02:47.380 --> 00:02:52.280
Yeah! It was more of an eclipse! Clouds
on a cloudy day are just sort of cloudy!

31
00:02:52.280 --> 00:02:53.560
Yeah, just sort of cloudy!

32
00:02:53.560 --> 00:02:54.390
Yeah, cloudy!

33
00:02:54.390 --> 00:02:56.480
Shut your gobs and listen!

34
00:02:56.520 --> 00:02:59.230
This cloud is called
a "cumuloregalis."

35
00:02:59.230 --> 00:03:00.400
It forms high in the sky.

36
00:03:00.460 --> 00:03:04.130
It has no internal air currents
and never turns to rain.

37
00:03:04.170 --> 00:03:05.540
No light can reach the ground,

38
00:03:05.590 --> 00:03:08.720
and a sunny afternoon
turns black as pitch.

39
00:03:08.760 --> 00:03:11.140
It's like some
giant hand reached up

40
00:03:11.140 --> 00:03:13.170
and snuffed out the
sun like it was a candle.

41
00:03:13.560 --> 00:03:17.150
Some say that cumuloregalis
clouds are floating fossils,

42
00:03:17.220 --> 00:03:18.930
petrified clouds
that hang in the sky

43
00:03:18.940 --> 00:03:21.430
unchanged for
thousands of years.

44
00:03:21.440 --> 00:03:23.320
You've gotta be kidding us!

45
00:03:23.360 --> 00:03:26.280
A petrified cloud that drifts
around for centuries?

46
00:03:26.300 --> 00:03:29.010
Hey, I'm just telling you
what people have told me.

47
00:03:29.060 --> 00:03:31.560
Whether you believe it
or not is entirely up to you.

48
00:03:31.570 --> 00:03:33.830
So it's a mystery cloud?

49
00:03:33.830 --> 00:03:37.170
Yeah, I guess that sums it up
about as well as anything else.

50
00:03:37.210 --> 00:03:38.880
This is so awesome!

51
00:03:38.920 --> 00:03:39.880
You see?

52
00:03:39.990 --> 00:03:41.540
There's only one place
this sky island can be

53
00:03:41.580 --> 00:03:44.970
if it's real and that's on top
of one of those clouds!

54
00:03:44.970 --> 00:03:46.140
Okay! Let's go!

55
00:03:46.140 --> 00:03:47.850
We're on our way up
to the clouds!

56
00:03:47.890 --> 00:03:49.310
Come on, Zoro, wake up!

57
00:03:49.380 --> 00:03:52.970
What? Is it morning already?

58
00:03:53.010 --> 00:03:54.970
Thanks for
all the information, Pops!

59
00:03:55.010 --> 00:03:56.180
Set sail!

60
00:03:56.220 --> 00:04:00.270
I told you idiots before,
we don't know how to get there!

61
00:04:00.310 --> 00:04:01.980
Yeah, that's gonna
be the hard part,

62
00:04:02.020 --> 00:04:04.110
'cause you'll be risking your
lives every minute!

63
00:04:04.150 --> 00:04:05.900
Y'mean like now?

64
00:04:06.070 --> 00:04:09.370
There's only one way to
get there: the Knock‐Up Stream.

65
00:04:09.410 --> 00:04:11.830
It shoots straight up in the
air, so it could theoretically

66
00:04:11.880 --> 00:04:14.090
take you into the
sky if you're lucky.

67
00:04:14.130 --> 00:04:16.180
Wait a minute, are
you trying to say this current

68
00:04:16.220 --> 00:04:17.760
can actually send ships flying?

69
00:04:17.800 --> 00:04:19.260
Great, then it'll be a snap!

70
00:04:19.410 --> 00:04:22.250
All we have to do
is hitch a ride up! Haha!

71
00:04:22.290 --> 00:04:24.540
Yahoo!

72
00:04:24.590 --> 00:04:25.670
On an ocean current?

73
00:04:25.680 --> 00:04:28.190
Except wouldn't we wind
up falling back into the ocean

74
00:04:28.190 --> 00:04:30.360
and getting smashed into pieces?

75
00:04:30.410 --> 00:04:31.740
Usually, that's true,

76
00:04:31.820 --> 00:04:33.800
unless you can get
the timing just right.

77
00:04:34.240 --> 00:04:35.620
It's one thing to
sit here all comfy

78
00:04:35.660 --> 00:04:38.830
and talk about riding
the Knock‐Up Stream.

79
00:04:38.870 --> 00:04:40.460
But if you think this
trip is gonna be

80
00:04:40.500 --> 00:04:41.750
some kind of pleasure cruise,

81
00:04:41.790 --> 00:04:43.670
you're in for a
very rude awakening.

82
00:04:43.670 --> 00:04:46.130
Oh...

83
00:04:46.170 --> 00:04:48.810
The Knock‐Up Stream is
as dangerous as any hurricane.

84
00:04:49.170 --> 00:04:51.850
It should be avoided
at all costs!

85
00:04:53.550 --> 00:04:55.640
But how exactly can
a current of water rise up

86
00:04:55.680 --> 00:04:57.390
in the air in the first place?

87
00:04:57.390 --> 00:04:58.640
Come on.

88
00:04:58.680 --> 00:05:01.390
We've never heard of anything
like that before, right, guys?

89
00:05:03.900 --> 00:05:05.650
I've heard a lot of
different explanations

90
00:05:05.770 --> 00:05:08.230
for the Knock‐Up Stream,
but there's only been one

91
00:05:08.270 --> 00:05:10.480
that's made any sense
to me, though so far

92
00:05:10.520 --> 00:05:13.900
no one has been stupid
enough to try and confirm it.

93
00:05:14.150 --> 00:05:16.650
It's like this.

94
00:05:19.490 --> 00:05:20.660
Deep below the ocean floor

95
00:05:20.670 --> 00:05:23.380
there are large cavities
filled with air.

96
00:05:23.420 --> 00:05:25.800
Cool seawater seeps
into these air pockets

97
00:05:25.840 --> 00:05:29.720
and geothermal energy heats
it until it becomes steam.

98
00:05:29.760 --> 00:05:31.930
Over time, the pressure builds.

99
00:05:31.970 --> 00:05:33.850
And when it finally
becomes too great,

100
00:05:33.870 --> 00:05:36.120
there's a tremendous explosion.

101
00:05:36.160 --> 00:05:38.370
This explosion hurls
the water into the air

102
00:05:38.420 --> 00:05:41.540
and creates a
current to the sky!

103
00:05:41.600 --> 00:05:43.810
The effect lasts
for about one minute.

104
00:05:43.820 --> 00:05:46.490
Connecting the ocean
to the sky like a bridge.

105
00:05:46.530 --> 00:05:47.440
One minute?

106
00:05:47.440 --> 00:05:49.650
Do you realize how
enormous an explosion

107
00:05:49.690 --> 00:05:51.000
like that would have to be?

108
00:05:51.240 --> 00:05:53.450
The explosions occur in
widely different locations

109
00:05:53.470 --> 00:05:55.600
about five times a month.

110
00:05:55.640 --> 00:05:59.220
We'd be blown into fish food
if we tried that, old man!

111
00:05:59.270 --> 00:06:01.390
Anyway, you can't
really explain something

112
00:06:01.430 --> 00:06:03.560
like this with
words and theories.

113
00:06:03.650 --> 00:06:05.820
It's one of the great
mysteries of creation.

114
00:06:05.820 --> 00:06:08.240
So... So then, we gotta
ride this current

115
00:06:08.280 --> 00:06:09.790
that shows up five times
a month into the air

116
00:06:09.830 --> 00:06:11.830
to reach some island in the sky

117
00:06:11.830 --> 00:06:14.370
and just hope
it's waiting for us?

118
00:06:14.420 --> 00:06:16.880
I'd say that
sums it up pretty well.

119
00:06:16.920 --> 00:06:20.340
And if you miss you fall back
down, slam into the ocean,

120
00:06:20.360 --> 00:06:22.860
and get blown into fish food.

121
00:06:22.900 --> 00:06:24.740
Just like you said.

122
00:06:27.500 --> 00:06:30.160
Okay! So we're gonna
forget about sky island, right?

123
00:06:31.950 --> 00:06:34.250
Because it's nuts, Luffy,
it's completely insane,

124
00:06:34.290 --> 00:06:36.290
you'd have to be luckier
than Mr. Lucky MacLuckluck

125
00:06:36.290 --> 00:06:39.890
of Luckyland on his lucky day to
pull off a stunt like this one!

126
00:06:42.290 --> 00:06:44.130
It'll be okay! Let's go!

127
00:06:44.170 --> 00:06:44.880
Naturally.

128
00:06:45.020 --> 00:06:46.480
How can you say it'll be okay?

129
00:06:46.520 --> 00:06:47.980
Think about it for a second!

130
00:06:48.020 --> 00:06:49.900
And what about
the poor Going Merry?

131
00:06:49.940 --> 00:06:52.570
Did you even stop to
consider the shape she's in?

132
00:06:52.570 --> 00:06:54.570
The way she's suffering?

133
00:06:54.600 --> 00:06:56.310
There's no way you can
expect her to stand up

134
00:06:56.340 --> 00:06:58.190
to a disaster like
this in her condition!

135
00:06:58.500 --> 00:07:00.670
He's absolutely right;
she's worthless.

136
00:07:00.700 --> 00:07:03.370
Even brand new that wreck
wouldn't stand a chance.

137
00:07:03.420 --> 00:07:04.550
Say what?

138
00:07:04.550 --> 00:07:06.550
Speed, weight, strength.

139
00:07:06.590 --> 00:07:08.470
The explosion would blow
that ship to pieces

140
00:07:08.510 --> 00:07:11.560
before it had a
chance to get airborne.

141
00:07:11.580 --> 00:07:12.780
Yeah.

142
00:07:12.820 --> 00:07:13.610
See?

143
00:07:13.650 --> 00:07:14.990
It's hopeless.

144
00:07:15.040 --> 00:07:17.260
But don't worry, I think
I can help you out there.

145
00:07:17.660 --> 00:07:20.750
I'll have Masira and Shojo do
a complete refit of your ship.

146
00:07:20.790 --> 00:07:22.670
I'll also have them
reinforce the superstructure

147
00:07:22.730 --> 00:07:23.940
to withstand the strain.

148
00:07:23.980 --> 00:07:24.810
You bet!

149
00:07:24.900 --> 00:07:26.900
Don't worry, guys,
we're all over it!

150
00:07:26.940 --> 00:07:28.480
Thanks, fellas!

151
00:07:28.500 --> 00:07:31.920
– Yeah, thanks a lot.
– Yeah, thanks a lot.

152
00:07:31.960 --> 00:07:33.670
Have you really
thought about this?

153
00:07:33.800 --> 00:07:34.960
What?

154
00:07:34.960 --> 00:07:36.880
Well, about... Oh, yeah,
how about the fact that at most,

155
00:07:36.920 --> 00:07:39.340
we can only stay one
more day on this island,

156
00:07:39.470 --> 00:07:40.930
or did that slip your mind?

157
00:07:40.950 --> 00:07:42.110
After that the
Log Pose will lock

158
00:07:42.180 --> 00:07:45.720
onto the next island's location,
and we'll have to move on.

159
00:07:45.840 --> 00:07:47.770
That's right! That's right!

160
00:07:47.770 --> 00:07:48.850
We gotta move on!

161
00:07:48.850 --> 00:07:49.980
Hey, old man!

162
00:07:50.010 --> 00:07:51.760
I realize you're not
a fortune teller

163
00:07:51.800 --> 00:07:53.600
and you probably don't
have the vaguest idea,

164
00:07:53.630 --> 00:07:55.380
but how many days will
it be until the next time

165
00:07:55.390 --> 00:07:57.480
the Knock‐Up Stream lines up
with the cumuloregalis,

166
00:07:57.520 --> 00:08:00.600
or better yet, how may weeks, or
months, or years, or centuries?

167
00:08:00.630 --> 00:08:02.050
Now, take your time,
I'm in no hurry.

168
00:08:02.090 --> 00:08:03.760
Can I lend you an abacus?

169
00:08:03.820 --> 00:08:05.320
It'll be at around
noon tomorrow,

170
00:08:05.350 --> 00:08:07.020
so you better get ready.

171
00:08:07.060 --> 00:08:08.940
Wait a minute,
you mean we can make it?

172
00:08:08.980 --> 00:08:10.020
Afraid so.

173
00:08:10.060 --> 00:08:11.850
If you're scared, then don't go.

174
00:08:14.360 --> 00:08:15.360
You're lying!

175
00:08:15.400 --> 00:08:16.240
Hmm?

176
00:08:16.280 --> 00:08:17.610
There's something
fishy about this!

177
00:08:17.660 --> 00:08:19.520
We've known you
less than 24 hours!

178
00:08:19.520 --> 00:08:21.430
Why are you being so helpful?

179
00:08:21.610 --> 00:08:22.740
And what's more?

180
00:08:22.800 --> 00:08:24.380
Now, hold on, Usopp!

181
00:08:24.430 --> 00:08:26.010
You hold on 'til I finish!

182
00:08:26.040 --> 00:08:28.500
This sky island,
this legendary place

183
00:08:28.540 --> 00:08:30.660
we don't even know the location
of is just gonna show up

184
00:08:30.660 --> 00:08:33.610
out of the blue
at noon tomorrow?

185
00:08:33.610 --> 00:08:34.780
And to help us get there.

186
00:08:34.830 --> 00:08:38.670
You're gonna rebuild and
reinforce our ship for nothing?

187
00:08:38.710 --> 00:08:41.660
When something looks too good
to be true, it probably is!

188
00:08:41.660 --> 00:08:42.620
The infamous Liar Noland

189
00:08:42.620 --> 00:08:45.060
is your great‐great
grand whatever, right?

190
00:08:45.060 --> 00:08:47.560
So how do we know
we can trust you?

191
00:08:49.920 --> 00:08:50.830
Hey, old man!

192
00:08:50.900 --> 00:08:53.900
Soup's on, come
and get it while it's hot!

193
00:08:54.060 --> 00:08:54.850
Move it!

194
00:08:54.850 --> 00:08:57.060
Nami, din...

195
00:08:57.940 --> 00:08:59.580
Hmm...

196
00:08:59.580 --> 00:09:00.960
Wha‐What is it?

197
00:09:01.110 --> 00:09:02.740
You wanna fight me?

198
00:09:02.780 --> 00:09:05.660
Masira said whenever
the "midday night" occurs

199
00:09:05.660 --> 00:09:08.410
in this area,
a cumuloregalis cloud appears

200
00:09:08.490 --> 00:09:11.450
in the southern
sky the next day.

201
00:09:11.790 --> 00:09:14.580
Based on its usual cycle
of five appearances a month

202
00:09:14.620 --> 00:09:16.040
I'm guessing the
Knock‐Up Stream

203
00:09:16.040 --> 00:09:18.710
will also erupt
tomorrow at midday.

204
00:09:18.750 --> 00:09:20.990
That will probably
also be to the south.

205
00:09:21.010 --> 00:09:22.720
I can't be 100 percent certain,

206
00:09:22.760 --> 00:09:26.880
but there's a good chance
the two events will coincide.

207
00:09:33.150 --> 00:09:36.570
I'm proud to have met
a bunch of brave fools like you.

208
00:09:36.580 --> 00:09:38.090
You're true pirates.

209
00:09:39.170 --> 00:09:41.710
Now then,
let's go inside and eat.

210
00:09:41.760 --> 00:09:46.340
Tonight, I want you to call
my home your home, my friends.

211
00:09:49.000 --> 00:09:50.660
Come on, Usopp. It's chow time!

212
00:09:50.660 --> 00:09:52.370
Yeah, come on you guys!

213
00:09:52.390 --> 00:09:54.100
Chopper, go get Robin!

214
00:09:54.660 --> 00:09:56.500
You bet!

215
00:09:57.330 --> 00:09:59.920
It looks like we're
going to this sky island,

216
00:09:59.960 --> 00:10:01.710
whether we're ready or not.

217
00:10:01.740 --> 00:10:04.000
Well let's just hope
our luck continues to hold.

218
00:10:04.040 --> 00:10:07.210
Nami, do you think
I'm a spineless little coward?

219
00:10:07.490 --> 00:10:09.150
You forgot to mention "idiot."

220
00:10:09.490 --> 00:10:10.990
And I know how you feel.

221
00:10:11.040 --> 00:10:13.210
Now why not go apologize?

222
00:10:15.170 --> 00:10:17.380
I'm sorry, Old‐timer!

223
00:10:17.440 --> 00:10:18.400
What are you doing?

224
00:10:18.440 --> 00:10:20.030
Don't wipe that nose on me!

225
00:10:20.030 --> 00:10:21.530
It's disgusting!

226
00:10:21.530 --> 00:10:22.650
Idiot!

227
00:10:25.010 --> 00:10:26.090
Come over here, buddy.

228
00:10:26.090 --> 00:10:27.720
Dance with me.

229
00:10:27.760 --> 00:10:29.550
Oh, yeah, keep it coming.

230
00:10:29.590 --> 00:10:31.010
That really hits the old spot!

231
00:10:31.050 --> 00:10:33.100
Eat up, everyone,
I'm just getting started.

232
00:10:33.140 --> 00:10:34.720
There's plenty more on the way!

233
00:10:34.770 --> 00:10:36.220
Here!

234
00:10:36.270 --> 00:10:37.570
Gotcha, ape face!

235
00:10:37.570 --> 00:10:38.370
Oh, yeah!

236
00:10:38.410 --> 00:10:40.790
It was hot sauce!

237
00:10:40.830 --> 00:10:41.870
Come here you squirrely,

238
00:10:41.870 --> 00:10:43.410
goggle eyed, little freak!

239
00:10:43.530 --> 00:10:44.240
Hey, good‐looking!

240
00:10:44.280 --> 00:10:45.240
You wanna sit next to me?

241
00:10:45.280 --> 00:10:46.450
Whoa, shift down
a gear, old man,

242
00:10:46.490 --> 00:10:49.750
you're about a hundred years
too old to be hitting on Nami!

243
00:10:58.170 --> 00:11:00.630
The skull's right eye.

244
00:11:00.690 --> 00:11:02.630
That's where I saw the gold.

245
00:11:02.630 --> 00:11:03.930
Gold?

246
00:11:07.300 --> 00:11:09.060
Those words, smeared with tears,

247
00:11:09.080 --> 00:11:12.540
are the last ones Noland
wrote in his log book.

248
00:11:12.620 --> 00:11:16.160
The skull's right eye,
that's where I saw the gold.

249
00:11:16.210 --> 00:11:19.200
That very same day,
Noland was executed.

250
00:11:19.920 --> 00:11:22.300
I still don't know
what he meant by that.

251
00:11:22.300 --> 00:11:24.130
Does it refer
to the name of a city,

252
00:11:24.180 --> 00:11:27.510
or some landmark that used
to exist on the island?

253
00:11:27.550 --> 00:11:31.060
Or is it just a poetic allusion
to his own impending death?

254
00:11:31.100 --> 00:11:34.140
The blank pages that
follow give us no clue.

255
00:11:35.610 --> 00:11:37.530
And that's the reason
we continue to dive

256
00:11:37.570 --> 00:11:39.160
to the bottom of the sea!

257
00:11:39.200 --> 00:11:40.790
We dream!

258
00:11:41.540 --> 00:11:43.120
Hooray for us!

259
00:11:45.120 --> 00:11:48.000
We're gonna go flying! Ha!

260
00:11:48.040 --> 00:11:50.630
Not if you keep
eating like that, we won't!

261
00:11:54.090 --> 00:11:56.410
Not if you keep
eating like that, we won't!

262
00:11:56.410 --> 00:11:58.540
but I can make out
the shoreline.

263
00:11:58.540 --> 00:11:59.460
It's clear.

264
00:11:59.500 --> 00:12:01.880
Drop anchor and prepare to land.

265
00:12:01.880 --> 00:12:06.670
Golden treasure shouldn't be
wasted on starry‐eyed dreamers.

266
00:12:08.980 --> 00:12:12.060
"Log entry for May 21st, 1122.

267
00:12:12.100 --> 00:12:15.230
Today I arrived on Jaya.

268
00:12:15.270 --> 00:12:17.400
Shortly after making landfall,
we were startled

269
00:12:17.440 --> 00:12:20.190
by the peculiar cries of
some strange bird from deep

270
00:12:20.240 --> 00:12:22.240
in the forest,
as well as the tolling

271
00:12:22.280 --> 00:12:25.120
of what seemed to
be an enormous bell.

272
00:12:25.120 --> 00:12:28.160
The ringing of that golden bell
resounded far and wide,

273
00:12:28.240 --> 00:12:30.370
as if announcing the
presence of some great

274
00:12:30.520 --> 00:12:32.900
and prosperous city of the past.

275
00:12:32.940 --> 00:12:34.770
It's fleeting echoes
the only reminder

276
00:12:34.820 --> 00:12:38.780
that this land was once home
to a mighty civilization."

277
00:12:41.490 --> 00:12:43.660
No land! No land!

278
00:12:43.700 --> 00:12:44.950
"In our pride and our arrogance,

279
00:12:44.950 --> 00:12:48.120
we thought we had seen and heard
everything in our short lives,

280
00:12:48.160 --> 00:12:51.460
but the sound of that bell
tolling left us all speechless.

281
00:12:51.460 --> 00:12:52.790
It was as if
the clang of that bell

282
00:12:52.830 --> 00:12:55.300
had struck us all
dumb with wonder!"

283
00:12:57.960 --> 00:13:00.710
Attaway, Noland!

284
00:13:00.790 --> 00:13:02.710
I just love stories
with bells in them,

285
00:13:02.750 --> 00:13:03.920
especially gold ones!

286
00:13:03.920 --> 00:13:04.970
Hey, Old‐timer, it sounds like

287
00:13:05.010 --> 00:13:08.300
you just might be little
fond of Noland after all!

288
00:13:09.750 --> 00:13:10.450
Look at this!

289
00:13:10.450 --> 00:13:12.770
Wow! A golden bell!

290
00:13:13.660 --> 00:13:15.690
Huh. Doesn't seem
too enormous to me.

291
00:13:16.630 --> 00:13:18.130
None of these is the one Noland

292
00:13:18.170 --> 00:13:19.800
was writing about
in his logbook.

293
00:13:19.820 --> 00:13:21.650
These are bell‐shaped
golden ingots we found

294
00:13:21.690 --> 00:13:23.490
on the ocean
floor during a dive.

295
00:13:23.560 --> 00:13:24.350
Golden ingots?

296
00:13:24.400 --> 00:13:26.900
The city of gold.
Hah! It is real!

297
00:13:26.950 --> 00:13:28.820
It's still not proof, though.

298
00:13:28.860 --> 00:13:31.450
You could find golden relics
like that lying around

299
00:13:31.510 --> 00:13:33.560
in any old ruin in
this part of the world.

300
00:13:33.600 --> 00:13:34.680
Maybe so,
but it does suggest

301
00:13:34.720 --> 00:13:37.980
there was a civilization
here some time in the past.

302
00:13:38.090 --> 00:13:39.480
Ingots are made to divide gold

303
00:13:39.480 --> 00:13:41.480
into standardized
units of weight.

304
00:13:41.520 --> 00:13:44.440
And that would indicate an
advanced trading society.

305
00:13:44.460 --> 00:13:46.500
Right, and you remember
that peculiar bird call

306
00:13:46.600 --> 00:13:48.240
he said they heard
when they landed?

307
00:13:48.240 --> 00:13:49.740
Show it to them!

308
00:13:51.660 --> 00:13:52.480
There's more gold?

309
00:13:52.480 --> 00:13:54.480
Just look at the size of it!

310
00:13:54.590 --> 00:13:55.590
That's the last of them.

311
00:13:55.630 --> 00:13:57.270
It's gorgeous!

312
00:13:57.620 --> 00:13:59.540
Golden bells and birds, huh?

313
00:13:59.560 --> 00:14:00.720
Do you think
they might be symbols

314
00:14:00.770 --> 00:14:02.890
of some ancient
Jayan civilization?

315
00:14:02.930 --> 00:14:05.640
I really couldn't say
for certain, but I'm pretty sure

316
00:14:05.690 --> 00:14:08.310
all these pieces were
part of the same casting.

317
00:14:08.450 --> 00:14:10.530
This creature is
called a South Bird.

318
00:14:10.620 --> 00:14:12.950
You can still find them
on this island today.

319
00:14:12.950 --> 00:14:15.040
Wow, and they
have a peculiar call?

320
00:14:15.040 --> 00:14:16.870
Uh‐huh. Just like
in the logbook.

321
00:14:16.910 --> 00:14:18.750
Sailors have
used South Birds on ships

322
00:14:18.840 --> 00:14:21.300
since ancient times
to help them nav...

323
00:14:24.150 --> 00:14:25.730
– Oh, no!
– Oh, no!

324
00:14:25.780 --> 00:14:26.470
What's wrong?

325
00:14:26.940 --> 00:14:27.900
This is really bad!

326
00:14:27.940 --> 00:14:28.810
You guys gotta get to the forest

327
00:14:28.810 --> 00:14:30.890
on the south side of
the island right away!

328
00:14:30.930 --> 00:14:32.770
Huh? What do you mean?

329
00:14:32.770 --> 00:14:33.730
Are you crazy?

330
00:14:33.770 --> 00:14:34.810
Don't waste a minute,

331
00:14:34.820 --> 00:14:36.660
you gotta capture one
of these birds and quickly!

332
00:14:36.680 --> 00:14:37.900
How come? Why?

333
00:14:37.940 --> 00:14:39.560
What do we need the bird for?

334
00:14:39.610 --> 00:14:41.190
Now listen carefully!

335
00:14:41.230 --> 00:14:43.480
Tomorrow you're going to
sail for the Knock‐Up Stream.

336
00:14:43.480 --> 00:14:45.190
It lies directly south of here.

337
00:14:45.240 --> 00:14:47.150
How are you going to get there?

338
00:14:49.620 --> 00:14:52.200
We just sail the ship
due south, of course.

339
00:14:52.260 --> 00:14:53.970
Have you forgotten
this is the Grand Line?

340
00:14:54.020 --> 00:14:55.570
You're heading for open sea!

341
00:14:55.610 --> 00:14:57.200
You won't be able
to get your bearings.

342
00:14:57.580 --> 00:14:58.830
Wait, he's right.

343
00:14:58.870 --> 00:15:01.870
Without a landmass to lock onto,
the Log Pose will be useless,

344
00:15:02.070 --> 00:15:03.510
and there'll be no way
for us to navigate.

345
00:15:04.070 --> 00:15:04.950
But there's got to be some way

346
00:15:04.950 --> 00:15:07.360
we can figure out
which direction to sail.

347
00:15:07.410 --> 00:15:10.470
There is if you can lay your
hands on one of these birds!

348
00:15:10.550 --> 00:15:12.260
Certain animals
are known to have

349
00:15:12.300 --> 00:15:14.430
a very accurate internal
sense of direction,

350
00:15:14.450 --> 00:15:16.250
as if they're born
with a natural compass.

351
00:15:17.670 --> 00:15:19.960
That makes Zoro
dumber than an animal!

352
00:15:20.330 --> 00:15:22.590
Oh yeah right, like
you have any room to talk.

353
00:15:22.630 --> 00:15:24.260
The South Bird
is a perfect example

354
00:15:24.300 --> 00:15:25.840
of this kind of creature.

355
00:15:27.800 --> 00:15:29.620
In the middle
of a vast, barren plain

356
00:15:29.620 --> 00:15:30.660
or a trackless ocean,

357
00:15:30.660 --> 00:15:34.660
its body will always point
in the same direction.

358
00:15:34.830 --> 00:15:36.500
Ohhh...

359
00:15:36.540 --> 00:15:37.540
You see?

360
00:15:37.540 --> 00:15:38.960
If you don't get going
to that forest and catch

361
00:15:39.000 --> 00:15:40.880
one of those birds right
now, you'll have to kiss

362
00:15:40.880 --> 00:15:42.960
you're only chance to get
to sky island goodbye!

363
00:15:43.000 --> 00:15:44.260
Aaah!

364
00:15:44.300 --> 00:15:46.720
And you're just
telling us this now?

365
00:15:46.880 --> 00:15:49.160
Quit your whining!

366
00:15:49.760 --> 00:15:51.010
There's no time for that now!

367
00:15:51.050 --> 00:15:51.890
Go!

368
00:15:51.930 --> 00:15:52.970
Move it or lose it!

369
00:15:53.010 --> 00:15:54.520
You expect us to go
stumbling through the forest

370
00:15:54.560 --> 00:15:55.720
in the middle of the night?

371
00:15:55.740 --> 00:15:57.400
Don't forget these!

372
00:15:58.820 --> 00:16:00.660
The three of us are gonna
get started on the repairs

373
00:16:00.790 --> 00:16:02.620
to your broken‐down ship.

374
00:16:02.670 --> 00:16:03.830
You know,
now that I think about it,

375
00:16:03.850 --> 00:16:05.810
we probably shouldn't
have had that party.

376
00:16:05.880 --> 00:16:08.010
Oh, sure, now you tell us!

377
00:16:08.010 --> 00:16:09.550
Listen! The clock's ticking.

378
00:16:09.550 --> 00:16:13.710
You have until morning to catch a South Bird
or you can forget about the whole thing!

379
00:16:14.290 --> 00:16:15.790
Whoa, it's pitch black!

380
00:16:15.830 --> 00:16:18.210
Will you let
go of me, you idiot?

381
00:16:21.150 --> 00:16:23.440
My stomach's still
so stuffed from that dinner.

382
00:16:24.970 --> 00:16:27.640
I couldn't catch
a bird if I saw one.

383
00:16:27.680 --> 00:16:29.680
So where are they?

384
00:16:30.140 --> 00:16:32.770
Let's just catch one of
'em and get back to drinkin'.

385
00:16:32.810 --> 00:16:35.770
Will someone remind me how
we ended up in this mess?

386
00:16:35.810 --> 00:16:36.650
Move it!

387
00:16:36.650 --> 00:16:37.980
Man, he couldn't
tell us in the daylight,

388
00:16:38.010 --> 00:16:40.590
it had to be the
middle of the night.

389
00:16:40.630 --> 00:16:41.510
Where are the birds?

390
00:16:41.620 --> 00:16:43.120
If I had any idea
where the birds were,

391
00:16:43.170 --> 00:16:45.460
do you think we'd out
here looking for them?

392
00:16:45.500 --> 00:16:47.750
All we know is this
thing's got a peculiar call.

393
00:16:47.750 --> 00:16:48.670
Yeah, and it looks

394
00:16:48.740 --> 00:16:50.700
just like that gold statue
he showed us.

395
00:16:50.710 --> 00:16:52.130
It's kinda hard
for me to believe any bird

396
00:16:52.170 --> 00:16:54.140
could be that screwy
looking in real life.

397
00:16:54.180 --> 00:16:56.970
Anyway, saying it has
a peculiar call is too vague,

398
00:16:57.040 --> 00:16:58.500
we can't I. D. it from that!

399
00:16:58.540 --> 00:17:00.000
Well, the old coot
didn't seem to think

400
00:17:00.060 --> 00:17:01.770
we'd have any
trouble finding one.

401
00:17:01.810 --> 00:17:03.810
Duuuuu!

402
00:17:03.850 --> 00:17:06.100
Hey! That sounded peculiar!

403
00:17:06.100 --> 00:17:07.060
Oh, ya think?

404
00:17:07.100 --> 00:17:08.190
Yeah!

405
00:17:08.230 --> 00:17:10.560
Well, we're here. Let's do it
and get it over with!

406
00:17:10.570 --> 00:17:11.720
Three nets, three teams.

407
00:17:11.720 --> 00:17:13.100
Let's split up
and bag that bird!

408
00:17:13.120 --> 00:17:14.330
– Right!
– Right!

409
00:17:14.360 --> 00:17:15.060
Let's go!

410
00:17:15.060 --> 00:17:18.350
We're gonna beat up a bird!

411
00:17:18.360 --> 00:17:20.850
We're trying to catch it!

412
00:17:24.770 --> 00:17:30.150
<b>Team 1
Sanji - Usopp - Nami</b>

413
00:17:26.540 --> 00:17:27.670
Don't worry, Nami‐baby,

414
00:17:27.800 --> 00:17:30.510
no matter what we run into,
I'm here to protect you.

415
00:17:30.550 --> 00:17:31.750
Me too, Sanji‐lump?

416
00:17:31.750 --> 00:17:34.380
Will you suck it up
and stop being such a wuss?

417
00:17:34.380 --> 00:17:35.340
Come on, guys,

418
00:17:35.380 --> 00:17:38.260
let's catch that bird
and get out of here!

419
00:17:38.300 --> 00:17:39.220
Okay, let's all settle down

420
00:17:39.260 --> 00:17:42.640
and see if we can hear
that bird's call again.

421
00:17:42.680 --> 00:17:44.270
Chee‐awww!

422
00:17:44.290 --> 00:17:45.790
Hey, there it is!

423
00:17:45.830 --> 00:17:47.380
Chee‐aw!

424
00:17:47.420 --> 00:17:48.550
Come on out!

425
00:17:48.550 --> 00:17:49.300
Moron!

426
00:17:49.350 --> 00:17:51.310
Chee‐aaw!

427
00:17:51.350 --> 00:17:52.890
Hey, why are you catching me?

428
00:17:52.970 --> 00:17:55.430
Huh? Hey, nice
accessorizing there, Nami!

429
00:17:55.470 --> 00:17:56.680
Where'd you get that thing?

430
00:17:56.720 --> 00:17:59.650
What thing? Ah!

431
00:17:59.650 --> 00:18:01.490
Spider!

432
00:18:02.950 --> 00:18:04.740
What, it's just a spider.

433
00:18:04.780 --> 00:18:06.370
Come here, you poor thing.

434
00:18:06.370 --> 00:18:08.290
I hate spiders, you idiot!

435
00:18:08.330 --> 00:18:09.920
Throw that thing away!

436
00:18:09.960 --> 00:18:12.500
Get rid of it, Usopp,
it's gotta be poisonous!

437
00:18:12.540 --> 00:18:15.250
Ahh! They're falling
from the trees!

438
00:18:15.400 --> 00:18:17.410
This is, just, too nasty.

439
00:18:17.730 --> 00:18:19.900
I bet you're a
species of tarantula!

440
00:18:19.950 --> 00:18:22.080
Don't worry, you guys,
they don't bite you

441
00:18:22.180 --> 00:18:23.780
unless you make them
angry or frighten them.

442
00:18:24.010 --> 00:18:26.220
I used to wrestle with spiders
like this all the time.

443
00:18:26.330 --> 00:18:27.750
Are you outta
your tiny little mind?

444
00:18:27.790 --> 00:18:30.250
Hairy bugs like that
completely freak me out!

445
00:18:30.300 --> 00:18:31.170
Yeah, me too!

446
00:18:31.210 --> 00:18:32.590
They make my flesh crawl!

447
00:18:32.640 --> 00:18:34.260
Throw it away, I can't stand it!

448
00:18:34.260 --> 00:18:35.130
Oh, really?

449
00:18:35.180 --> 00:18:36.340
But you don't seem
to have any problem

450
00:18:36.390 --> 00:18:39.600
with those centipedes and
moths on the tree behind you.

451
00:18:46.580 --> 00:18:47.920
I hate those things too!

452
00:18:50.410 --> 00:18:52.750
Wait! Don't leave me here alone!

453
00:18:52.760 --> 00:18:54.720
Wait!

454
00:18:54.110 --> 00:18:57.240
<b>Team 2
Zoro - Robin</b>

455
00:18:54.730 --> 00:18:56.570
That sounded like
someone screaming.

456
00:18:56.640 --> 00:18:57.890
Really?

457
00:19:02.520 --> 00:19:05.360
A centipede, and a big one.

458
00:19:05.400 --> 00:19:07.570
You don't have to kill every
living thing we meet,

459
00:19:07.610 --> 00:19:08.940
it's kind of rude.

460
00:19:08.970 --> 00:19:12.560
Yeah, well, he shouldn't
have snuck up on me.

461
00:19:12.600 --> 00:19:13.470
Hey, wait!

462
00:19:13.520 --> 00:19:14.560
Now what?

463
00:19:14.660 --> 00:19:17.080
You're going back
the way we came.

464
00:19:17.080 --> 00:19:19.620
Duuuuu!

465
00:19:19.640 --> 00:19:20.760
There it is again!

466
00:19:20.830 --> 00:19:22.750
Come on!

467
00:19:22.780 --> 00:19:25.700
And be careful not
to step in the quicksand.

468
00:19:27.240 --> 00:19:28.950
Hey! Wait for...

469
00:19:33.430 --> 00:19:34.400
I gotcha!

470
00:19:35.700 --> 00:19:40.280
<b>Team 3
Luffy - Chopper</b>

471
00:19:35.950 --> 00:19:37.110
It's an Atlas!

472
00:19:37.210 --> 00:19:38.130
Atlas?

473
00:19:38.170 --> 00:19:40.090
That's right. This one
and the Hercules beetle

474
00:19:40.130 --> 00:19:41.710
are some of the
most prized insects

475
00:19:41.750 --> 00:19:43.470
in the world for collectors!

476
00:19:43.510 --> 00:19:45.540
You mean there are
people who collect insects?

477
00:19:45.540 --> 00:19:46.500
Oh, yeah!

478
00:19:46.540 --> 00:19:48.080
And these guys are some
of the hardest to catch!

479
00:19:48.080 --> 00:19:50.840
Hey, which one is cooler,
that bug or the One Piece?

480
00:19:50.880 --> 00:19:53.160
Ah! That's a tough one!

481
00:19:53.160 --> 00:19:55.410
Luffy!
There's one over here too!

482
00:19:55.420 --> 00:19:56.120
Oh, yeah!

483
00:19:56.120 --> 00:19:57.950
A Miyama stag beetle!

484
00:19:57.980 --> 00:19:59.480
Gotta catch it!

485
00:20:00.310 --> 00:20:01.690
He flew away!

486
00:20:03.060 --> 00:20:05.610
Duuuuu!

487
00:20:05.650 --> 00:20:08.070
After him!
Don't let him get away!

488
00:20:09.110 --> 00:20:11.860
A beehive! Run!

489
00:20:23.920 --> 00:20:27.500
What the heck
is wrong with this forest?

490
00:20:27.550 --> 00:20:29.500
Luffy, look up there!

491
00:20:29.550 --> 00:20:30.420
The bird!

492
00:20:30.560 --> 00:20:31.980
He's the one
who dropped the beehives!

493
00:20:32.020 --> 00:20:34.520
Duuu! Duuu!

494
00:20:34.650 --> 00:20:35.900
What'd he say?
What'd he say?

495
00:20:35.970 --> 00:20:38.050
Did you understand him?

496
00:20:38.100 --> 00:20:39.220
Uhh.

497
00:20:39.220 --> 00:20:42.140
He said, "I'll kill anyone
who disturbs this forest!"

498
00:20:53.860 --> 00:20:54.990
Now, now, little boy,

499
00:20:55.030 --> 00:20:57.780
you don't really want to go
and make us angry, do you?

500
00:20:57.820 --> 00:20:59.950
It's not nice to show
up in the middle of the night

501
00:21:00.160 --> 00:21:02.370
and demand gold from someone.

502
00:21:02.800 --> 00:21:04.760
Now get lost,
we've got a lot of work to do

503
00:21:04.800 --> 00:21:06.100
and not a lot of time to do it.

504
00:21:07.350 --> 00:21:09.140
You must be the bodyguards.

505
00:21:09.140 --> 00:21:12.230
And the old man must be
the ex‐pirate Cricket.

506
00:21:12.290 --> 00:21:13.210
I heard the sad story

507
00:21:13.250 --> 00:21:15.960
of the Mont Blanc family
back at the tavern.

508
00:21:15.960 --> 00:21:17.920
It moved me to tears.

509
00:21:17.940 --> 00:21:21.390
But stealing another man's
treasure moves me even more.

510
00:21:21.390 --> 00:21:23.720
I'm a pirate. Ha!

511
00:21:23.770 --> 00:21:24.730
Cricket!

512
00:21:24.730 --> 00:21:26.060
Maybe you'd like to
know what people call me

513
00:21:26.100 --> 00:21:28.150
before I take your
whole life's work.

514
00:21:28.190 --> 00:21:30.190
The Hyena!
